\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Testing nontriviality through split inclusions.} An elementary operator-algebra mechanism would work if the wedge inclusion admitted a spatial tensor representation
\[
 A(W_1)\subset B(\mathcal K)\otimes I\subset A(W_2)\subset B(\mathcal K\otimes\mathcal L),
\]
and if $A(W_2)$ also contained $I\otimes B(\mathcal L)$ with $\dim\mathcal L>1$. Under these explicit hypotheses $I\otimes B(\mathcal L)\subset A(W_1)'\cap A(W_2)$, and any nontrivial projection on $\mathcal L$ gives a bounded nonscalar local observable. Commutation is immediate on elementary tensors and extends by continuity.

\paragraph{Why an intermediate type-I factor alone is insufficient.} Set $\mathcal K=\mathbb C^2$, $\mathcal L=\mathbb C^2$, and $A(W_1)=A(W_2)=B(\mathcal K)\otimes I$. There is a type-I factor between the two algebras, but the relative commutant inside $A(W_2)$ is only $\mathbb C I$. Indeed writing an element as $X\otimes I$, commutation with all matrix units forces $X$ scalar. The preceding sufficient criterion specifically needs independent operators in the larger algebra, not just an ambient tensor decomposition.

\paragraph{Attempt outcome.} This exposes the first missing implication in a bare ``split therefore nontrivial'' argument. The source's modular nuclearity method has additional vacuum and standardness information; none of it is proved by the toy inclusion. To treat the full factorizing-S-matrix family one must obtain those structural hypotheses from the actual wedge representation, or construct a nonscalar element directly. No result for an S-matrix outside the already known subfamily is established here.

